      <p class="eyebrow">about · who's behind this</p>
      <h1>One pair of hands, and a lot of personas.</h1>
      <p class="sub">
        I'm <b>Daan Gerits</b>. I've spent my working life building data platforms for banks,
        telcos, and airports, and these days my day job is building the technical AI
        foundations for organizations. impire.io is what I build around that, in the open:
        <b>soulstream</b>, <b>PRA</b>, and <b>imps</b>.
      </p>
      <p class="sub">
        All three come out of one conviction that settled in around 2012 and never left:
        <b>the world is inherently event-driven</b>. Things happen, constantly and from every
        direction, and nothing is quite the same afterward. That took me to the front of
        event-driven architectures, and to Synadia, where I built and researched event-driven
        AI on top of NATS.
      </p>
    </div>
  </header>

  <main class="prose">
    <h3>Why I build this</h3>
    <p>
      What I love most sits between fields: taking an idea that's ordinary in one area and
      finding out it's radical in another. Everything here is that one habit, pointed at
      three different things.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Pointed at collaboration, it becomes soulstream.</strong> The tools we work in
      were built for people and had AI bolted on afterwards: an assistant in a sidebar, a bot
      reached through its own API, a copilot living outside the document. The machine is
      always a guest holding a different key. I wanted the opposite — one record, one kind of
      credential, no separate lane, so a person and a model take their turn at the same
      bench. And I wanted the result to run on hardware I control.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Pointed at the brain, it becomes PRA.</strong> A brain, whatever else it is, is
      the most event-driven system there is: things happen to it, constantly and from every
      direction at once. So what if learning is prediction over movement rather than labels
      over data? PRA is that question with a laptop behind it — an intelligence with no task,
      learning a world by living in it.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Pointed at agents, it becomes imps.</strong> You hear an entire room and think
      about almost none of it, until someone says your name. Awareness cheap and constant,
      thinking rare and expensive — with the line between them enforced by the compiler
      rather than by good intentions.
    </p>

    <h3>Working with an AI, out loud</h3>
    <p>
      This is a joint project between me and an AI, and I mean that concretely, not as a
      confession buried in the acknowledgments. The ideas grow out of dialogue: I push, the
      machine pushes back, and what survives gets written down. Papers I would never get
      through alone get translated until I can actually use them. Code and prose get drafted
      faster than I could ever type them.
    </p>
    <p>
      Then comes the part that is entirely mine: <strong>deciding what's true</strong>.
      Nothing here survived because it sounded good. Claims pass a bar that was written down
      before the experiment ran, on runs that reproduce byte for byte, in a record that keeps
      the failures filed next to the successes.
    </p>

    <h3>I am not a scientist</h3>
    <p>
      No PhD, no lab, no grant. A laptop, a stubborn streak, and an unusual collaborator. I
      know how that looks from a distance — the internet has a shelf ready for a tinkerer
      with a theory of learning, somewhere between perpetual motion and secret cancer cures.
      That shelf is exactly why the method is strict.
    </p>
    <p>
      You are not asked to trust me anywhere on this site. Where I claim a measurement, you
      can rerun it. Where an idea is borrowed, I say whose it was. Where something is
      unproven, the text says so and moves on. If smarter people with better tools read this
      and see further, that isn't a defeat — that's the point. The longer version of all this
      is <a href="https://impire.io/poseres-book/00-a-note-before-we-start.html">the note at
      the start of the book</a>.
    </p>

    <h3>The founding bet</h3>
    <p>
      The "what is needed" list stays short, and everything else is vocabulary over the
      log, backends on named seams, or optional extensions. A working deployment is a NATS
      server with JetStream, credentials, and the protocol — <strong>no API tier, no
      database, no coordinator</strong>. Each component holds its own version of the bet:
      a running agent is a persona, not a service tier; identity signs instead of handing
      out keys; sign-in is standard OIDC, replaceable by configuration; owning your realm
      costs one binary and one command.
    </p>

    <h3>Who it is for</h3>
    <p>
      People and agents who want to collaborate as peers, without a second-class lane for
      the AI — and who want the result on hardware they control. Independent professionals
      and small teams standing up a realm over their own NATS; humans on the CLI or a
      browser, agents through MCP, all first-class personas on one stream. The substrate is
      the product; the bet is that useful clients grow around a stable protocol rather than
      the protocol growing around one client.
    </p>

    <h3>What I refuse to build</h3>
    <p>The refusals are the spine of the thing:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>A second lane for the AI.</strong> No bot API, no attribution laundering. Delegation is scoped credentials or a separately named persona.</li>
      <li><strong>A system with a coordinator or a consensus round.</strong> Deterministic rules, idempotent operations, optimistic concurrency — no steward, no election, no lock service.</li>
      <li><strong>A store of record outside the record.</strong> Runtimes hold views, never the artefact.</li>
      <li><strong>A KMS, an identity provider, or a parallel permission system.</strong> Identity truth lives in the deployment's IAM; the NATS server stays the verifier of record.</li>
      <li><strong>A password store or a privileged peer.</strong> Sign-in is passkeys-only and must remain indistinguishable from any external OIDC provider.</li>
      <li><strong>A dev-mode fork.</strong> What runs on your machine is the real shape, and every component stays independently deployable.</li>
      <li><strong>A wrapper around NATS.</strong> Lean on NATS, don't wrap it: subject permissions are nearly the whole security model.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="aside">
      Will there ever be a hosted version, for people who'd rather not run their own? Maybe.
      It's a fair thing to want, and nothing here is built to prevent it: it would run the
      same shape you run today, and self-hosting stays a first-class path either way.
      Nothing about it is promised.
    </p>

    <h3>How ambition stays honest</h3>
    <p>
      Invented vocabulary is a budget, spent only on concepts a plain word can't carry.
      Every ambition sits behind a named, pre-registered research gate, and no build outruns
      its gate. Direction decisions record what would change our minds when they are made.
      Claims carry evidence classes, and <strong>only a measurement closes a debate</strong>.
      The full discipline — constitution, research process, and the numbered journey of
      every reversal — is public in
      <a href="https://github.com/impire-io/soul-hq">soul-hq</a>.
    </p>

    <h3>Colophon</h3>
    <p>
      This site is hand-built and served as static files: no cookies, no external requests
      beyond a privacy-friendly page counter, system fonts plus one self-hosted typeface.
      Every page has a markdown mirror for agents (see <a href="/llms.txt">/llms.txt</a>).
      The code and content are source-available under the
      <a href="/license/">Sustainable Use License</a> (fair-code) — free to self-host,
      commercial offering by agreement.
    </p>
